---
title: Files & sidecars
description: What Latent writes to disk, what it leaves alone, and how to move a catalogue or back it up.
---

Latent is non-destructive in the literal sense: it does not rewrite your RAW files. What it writes
instead is a **sidecar** — a separate file beside the original, holding the edit stack for that
frame. The Info panel reports the sidecar's state per frame, so you can see whether the current
edit has been written out.

Your folders stay the organising principle. There is no import step that copies photos into a
library of Latent's own, which means a folder of RAWs plus its sidecars is the complete,
self-contained record of the work.

## What that gets you

- **The original is always recoverable.** Delete the sidecar and the frame is back to how the
  camera wrote it.
- **Edits travel with the photos.** Copy a folder to another machine, sidecars included, and the
  work comes with it.
- **Backups are ordinary file backups.** Whatever you already use for the RAWs covers the edits,
  because they are just more files in the same directory.

## Identifying frames

Alongside the metadata it reads from the file, Latent records a content hash per frame. That is
what lets it recognise a photo as the same photo after it has been moved or renamed, rather than
treating it as something new.

## Storing the results

Latent itself stores nothing for you — it edits what is on your disk and stops there. Keeping the
results safe is a separate job, and one a NeoWorks plan will do: encrypted
[storage](/docs/neoworks/encryption) with the keys derived on your device, so the platform holds
ciphertext it cannot read. The [encryption model](/docs/neoworks/encryption) covers how that
works.
